From: MicroBlaze toolchain integration
Subject: [PATCH] libstdc++: build fast_float without a 128-bit integer type

fast_float infers a 64-bit platform from SIZE_MAX when it does not recognise the
architecture, which is the case for MicroBlaze-64, and then full_multiplication
requires __uint128_t with "#error Not implemented" as the only alternative.
MB-64 has no TImode support, so libstdc++ cannot be built for the m64 multilibs.

The file already carries a portable 64x64 -> 128 multiplication, used on 32-bit
targets. Make it available whenever no 128-bit integer type exists (except under
Visual Studio, which supplies _umul128 as an intrinsic) and select it in
full_multiplication. scalar_mul already routes through full_multiplication when
__SIZEOF_INT128__ is undefined, so nothing else needs changing.

This is a portability fix rather than a MicroBlaze one: any 64-bit target without
__int128 hits it.

Apply to the GCC tree.

Upstream-Status: Pending

--- a/libstdc++-v3/src/c++17/fast_float/fast_float.h
+++ b/libstdc++-v3/src/c++17/fast_float/fast_float.h
@@ -241,7 +241,9 @@
 #endif
 }
 
-#ifdef FASTFLOAT_32BIT
+// A 64-bit target without a 128-bit integer type needs the emulation too.
+#if defined(FASTFLOAT_32BIT) \
+    || (!defined(__SIZEOF_INT128__) && !defined(FASTFLOAT_VISUAL_STUDIO))
 
 // slow emulation routine for 32-bit
 fastfloat_really_inline uint64_t emulu(uint32_t x, uint32_t y) {
@@ -263,7 +265,7 @@
 }
 #endif // !__MINGW64__
 
-#endif // FASTFLOAT_32BIT
+#endif // FASTFLOAT_32BIT || no 128-bit integer type
 
 
 // compute 64-bit a*b
@@ -278,10 +280,13 @@
 #elif defined(FASTFLOAT_32BIT) ||                                              \
     (defined(_WIN64) && !defined(__clang__) && !defined(_M_ARM64))
   answer.low = _umul128(a, b, &answer.high); // _umul128 not available on ARM64
-#elif defined(FASTFLOAT_64BIT)
+#elif defined(FASTFLOAT_64BIT) && defined(__SIZEOF_INT128__)
   __uint128_t r = ((__uint128_t)a) * b;
   answer.low = uint64_t(r);
   answer.high = uint64_t(r >> 64);
+#elif !defined(FASTFLOAT_VISUAL_STUDIO)
+  // 64-bit target with no 128-bit integer type.
+  answer.low = _umul128(a, b, &answer.high);
 #else
   #error Not implemented
 #endif
